    "case_id": "714",
    "case_summary": "An 85-year-old man was admitted due to skin ulceration in the left upper limb, lethargy and fever for 1 day. One day prior, he was stabbed in the left upper limb while cleaning sea shrimps and then developed drowsiness and fever, with a maximum body temperature of 38.8°C. The patient had a history of diabetes for more than 20 years and a long-term history of alcoholism.\n\nPatient was lethargic, with low spirit. Left hand and forearm showed:- Large areas of fluid exudation- Local tension blisters- Dark purple ecchymoses at the base- Slightly cool ends of the five fingers- Extensive swelling from proximal middle segment to wrist- Scattered petechiae and ecchymosis on skin of whole body\n\nLaboratory Tests:- White blood cell count: 11.77 × 109/L- Procalcitonin: > 100 ng/mL- Lactate: 4.28 mmol/LMicrobiology:- Blood culture: Positive for Gram-negative bacilli- NGS analysis of blood sample performed- Microscopic examination showed comma-like, flagellate, spore-free bacteria- Culture on blood agar showed round, slightly convex, moist, gray, opaque and grass-green β-hemolytic coloniesImages:Figure 1A: Shows left upper limb swelling with tension blisters and dark purple ecchymosesFigure 1D: Direct microscopic examination of bacteria in blood cultureFigure 1E: Colony growth in blood agar plateFigure 1F: NGS technology detection results",
